# Working agreements

This repository is the reference implementation of the client side of the Accountable Inference
Delivery Protocol (AIRP), described in a paper by Justin Philip Flores. The code exists to be an
existence proof and to be read. Optimize for legibility over cleverness.

Read `ARCHITECTURE.md` before making structural changes. It maps every module to the paper
section and step it implements, and it lists what the build deliberately does not have.

## Rules that are not negotiable

**Every module states its paper section and step at the top of the file.** If you add a module,
add that header. If you change what a module does, change the header with it. The code and the
paper are meant to stay in register, and that comment is the register.

**No em-dashes in any prose file.** Not in markdown, not in comments, not in strings. Use
commas, periods, colons or parentheses. This applies to anything you write in this repository.

**Data before code.** Taxonomies, delivery policy, jurisdiction rulesets, the serving register
and the standing document are versioned documents under `data/`, not constants in TypeScript.
Whose values define a flag is an open question the paper puts in Section 9, and that argument
cannot be held over a compiled constant. Do not move any of it into code.

**Honesty over polish.** The advocate reports its own gaps at startup and the UI renders them.
When you add a mechanism that is partial, say so in the code, in `ARCHITECTURE.md` under "What
this build does not have", and in the runtime warnings if a user could otherwise assume it is
complete. A reference implementation that overstates itself is worse than none.

**Every claim the code makes about itself needs a test that would fail if it stopped being
true.** The two that matter most are in `packages/core/test/store.test.ts`, which reads raw
SQLite rows and asserts conversation content is not in the ledger, and
`packages/core/test/telemetry.test.ts`, which asserts a telemetry batch contains no words.

**Small, message-disciplined commits.** The history is meant to read as a build narrative.

**Refresh the public demo when UI or daemon changes are viewable.** This host is the production
demo site. When changes are ready to look at (committed or not), rebuild what the daemon serves
and restart it so the live site updates:

```bash
npm run build:ui          # when packages/ui changed
# also rebuild/restart if packages/daemon or core packages the daemon loads changed
pm2 restart airp-daemon
```

The daemon (`airp-daemon` under PM2) serves `packages/ui/dist` from `.advocate-public` on
127.0.0.1:8790. Do not leave the user staring at a stale build.

## Structural boundaries

- `packages/core` has no UI dependency and no framework, and reaches the network only to talk
  to configured providers and the configured evaluator. Keep it that way.
- `packages/daemon` binds to 127.0.0.1 only. It is the seam between a core that needs a
  filesystem and a UI that runs in a browser tab. Desktop packaging replaces it later.
- The telemetry emitter is constructed with a ledger-scoped key and nothing else, which is what
  makes "it cannot transmit conversation content" a structural fact rather than a promise.
  Adding a transcript dependency to that class breaks the central privacy claim of the paper.
- Evidence spans are conversation content, so they live in the transcript store under the
  transcript key, and the ledger holds only an opaque reference. Do not move them.

## Style

TypeScript, strict, ES modules, `.js` extensions on relative imports because the build targets
NodeNext. No runtime dependencies: `node:sqlite` for persistence and `node:crypto` for signing.
Adding a runtime dependency to `packages/core` is a decision worth arguing for out loud, not a
convenience.

Comments explain why, not what. Several files in this repository carry a paragraph of reasoning
at the top. That is deliberate and it is the house style.
